.TH CVECTOR_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at a vector's first element
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cmap_iterator *cvector_begin_iter(cvec v, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator positioned at element 0 that walks the vector in
index order. The key of the iterator is the zero-based index of the
element, and its value is the element itself. The caller reads both with
the unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3),
which give typed pointers straight into the backing buffer of the
vector, so a write through the value pointer updates the element in
place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a cvec and is
the ordinary entry point. Call this function directly only when the
container type is already known and the caller wants the error string.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Vector to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer to receive an error string on failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a newly allocated iterator positioned at the first
element.
Returns NULL when there is nothing to iterate (v is NULL or the vector
holds no elements), with *err set to NULL.
Returns NULL with *err set to an error string when the iterator itself
could not be allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL at entry, before anything else. To tell
an empty result apart from a failure, test *err, not the return value
A NULL v iterates as an empty vector instead of reporting an error. This
is a deliberate cross-module contract that chashmap_begin_iter(3) and
cbmap_begin_iter(3) share: the caller can iterate a lazily created
container field that is still NULL because nothing is in it yet, and no
caller needs to write its own NULL guard first
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
element and returns NULL. An iteration that stops early must end with
ccol_iter_destroy(3), or the caller can declare the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3), so that scope exit destroys it
The iterator comes from the vector's own memory management procedures
Every operation that inserts, removes or resizes invalidates the
iterator
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 10);
cvec_push(vec, 20);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(vec, it);
for (it = cvector_begin_iter(vec, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("[%zu] = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                            *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3)
